      "source_id": "x:geoffreylitt",
      "type": "x",
      "name": "Geoffrey Litt",
      "items": [
        {
          "published_at": "2026-11-07T04:16:10.384Z",
          "url": "https://x.com/geoffreylitt/status/2118920419839557658",
          "topic": "Preinfusion as a research and project-planning workflow",
          "text": "One of my favorite research practices I learned at @inkandswitch is “preinfusion”. The term comes from espresso (I believe @pvh coined it?) - and the original meaning is that you have a lower pressure soak before you turn on the high pressure and pull the shot. \n\nThe way it works at the lab is that you patiently let a cluster of ideas slowly form and develop, meandering somewhat in the background, and you lay the groundwork to work on them more seriously… before starting an intense high-pressure sprint. Could be a few months of part-time preinfusion before a hardcore 3 month project.\n\nThis really helps avoid the problem where you’re trying to sprint, but you don’t actually have enough clarity to know where to run.\n\nI think this applies in many other contexts. Preinfuse before trying to write an essay. Preinfuse before raising money for a company. A slow and patient preparation can enable swift follow through.",
          "attachments": [],
          "quote_tweet": null,
          "author_replies": [],
